---
import Project from '../layouts/Project.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Panel from '../components/Panel.astro';
import Badge from '../components/Badge.astro';
import { project } from '../data/projects';
const p = project('Sentinel');
const repo = p.repo;
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is Sentinel?',
    answer: 'Sentinel is an open-source security layer and registry for npm. It audits package tarballs before install-time code runs and returns a deterministic allow, warn, or block verdict.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Sentinel protect AI agents that install npm packages?',
    answer: 'Sentinel places policy and human approval between an agent request and package execution. The agent can request an exception, but only a human can grant one.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Sentinel modify packages from npm?',
    answer: 'Its transparent proxy serves upstream packages unchanged while attaching audit findings and a verdict. The registry layer separately supports audit-gated publishing and verified namespace claims.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Sentinel production ready?',
    answer: 'Sentinel is a pre-1.0 alpha (currently 0.1.0-alpha.2), it is not production hardened, and its APIs may change. Codento Engine runs it in production as an auditing npm proxy in observe mode, where it scores every tarball and blocks none. Enforcement (block) mode is not yet proven in production.',
  },
];
const docs = [
  { label: 'README', href: `${repo}#readme` },
  { label: 'Architecture', href: `${repo}/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md` },
  { label: 'ADRs', href: `${repo}/tree/main/docs/adr` },
  { label: 'Threat model', href: `${repo}/blob/main/sentinel-threat-model.md` },
  { label: 'Security policy', href: `${repo}/blob/main/SECURITY.md` },
  { label: 'Contributing', href: `${repo}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md` },
  { label: 'License', href: `${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE` },
];
---
<Project
  project={p}
  title="npm Supply-Chain Security for AI Agents | Sentinel"
  description="Audit npm packages before install-time code runs. Sentinel gives AI agents deterministic risk verdicts, sandbox enforcement, and human approval gates."
  heading="npm supply-chain security for AI agents."
  softwareDescription="Open-source npm supply-chain security and registry infrastructure for AI agents."
  programmingLanguage="TypeScript"
  faqs={faqs}
  docs={docs}
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Sentinel is an auditing proxy in front of registry.npmjs.org. It serves
    real packages unchanged, scores every tarball with a deterministic audit
    engine and attaches a verdict, so an agent or a human sees the risk
    before install-time code runs. It also works as a registry, with
    audit-gated publishes and verified namespace ownership.
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="status">
    <div><dt>Maturity</dt><dd>Pre-1.0 alpha. Not production hardened. APIs may change.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Latest release</dt><dd>0.1.0-alpha.2 on npm, released 14 July 2026.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Production use</dt><dd>The auditing npm proxy in Codento Engine's build stack, in observe mode. There are no other production users.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Not yet done</dt><dd>Enforcement (block) mode is not yet proven in production. The November 2026 fixes are on <code>main</code> and not in a release yet.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Language</dt><dd>TypeScript</dd></div>
    <div><dt>License</dt><dd>Apache 2.0</dd></div>
  </Fragment>

  <Section id="quickstart" title="Quickstart" note="Installs from npm.">
    <p>
      The current alpha is 0.1.0-alpha.2. All seven
      <code>@git-agentic/sentinel-*</code> packages are published under the
      <code>alpha</code> dist-tag, and the <code>latest</code> tag points to
      the same version.
    </p>
    <Panel title="shell" copy>
<pre><code>$ npm install -g @git-agentic/sentinel-cli@alpha @git-agentic/sentinel-proxy@alpha

<span class="comment"># audit a package version before anything installs it</span>
$ sentinel audit is-odd 3.0.1</code></pre>
    </Panel>
  </Section>

  <Section id="features" title="Features" note="npm cannot retract bad releases, has no install-time permissions and lets attackers squat names. Agents install with no risk signal.">
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="card feature feature--wide">
        <div class="badges"><Badge kind="prod" /></div>
        <h3>Auditing proxy</h3>
        <p>
          The proxy resolves and serves real npm packages unchanged. It
          intercepts every tarball, scores it and returns the verdict in
          response headers. Under a <code>block</code> policy a bad tarball
          becomes a 403 at install time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Codento Engine runs this proxy in its build stack in observe mode.
          It scores every tarball and blocks none yet. Block mode is not yet
          proven in production.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Deterministic scoring</h3>
        <p>A score starts at 100 and loses a weighted penalty per finding. Any critical finding forces a block. The result is reproducible in CI.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Policy and human approval</h3>
        <p>Policy is signed per enterprise. An agent can request an approval, and only a human can grant it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Deny-by-default sandbox</h3>
        <p>Lifecycle scripts run with no network, no credential environment variables and no writes outside a fixed floor, on macOS Seatbelt or Linux bubblewrap and Landlock.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Audit-gated publish</h3>
        <p>An npm-compatible publish route scores every version before it is accepted and commits atomically.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Verified namespace claims</h3>
        <p>An organization proves ownership of a name with a DNS TXT challenge on its apex domain. A claimed name is served only from Sentinel's own store.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Time-locked retraction</h3>
        <p>A publisher can pull a bad release only while it is younger than 72 hours and under 1,000 cumulative downloads.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Whole-tree audits</h3>
        <p>Audit every package in an npm, yarn or pnpm lockfile, write a CycloneDX SBOM, and produce signed attestations that verify offline.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Agent and CI interfaces</h3>
        <p>A stdio MCP server for agent hosts, a CLI whose exit codes make <code>sentinel audit</code> a CI gate, and a GitHub Action that posts the verdict to the pull request.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="how-it-works" title="How it works">
    <h3>The demo</h3>
    <p>
      A previously clean package ships a patch release with a
      <code>postinstall</code> script that harvests secrets. This is the
      event-stream pattern, and it still works against plain npm. Below is
      Sentinel's verdict, and what <code>npm install</code> sees when it
      fetches the bad tarball under a block policy.
    </p>
    <Panel title="sentinel audit">
<pre><code>$ sentinel audit color-stream 1.4.1

  color-stream@1.4.1
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  install    ⚠ runs lifecycle scripts
  score      ░░░░░░░░░░ 0/100
  verdict    <span class="bad">BLOCK</span>
  findings (7)
  <span class="bad">critical</span> [install-scripts] postinstall reads environment variables,
                              decodes an encoded blob
  <span class="bad">critical</span> [secret-exfil]    reads sensitive material (~/.npmrc, AWS
                              credentials) with a network egress sink
  <span class="bad">high</span>     [network-egress]  connects to a hardcoded IP address
  <span class="bad">high</span>     [obfuscation]     uses eval()

$ npm install --registry http://localhost:4873 color-stream
  HTTP 403  x-sentinel-verdict: block  x-sentinel-score: 0

<span class="comment"># and a clean package passes untouched</span>
$ sentinel audit is-odd 3.0.1
<span class="ok">  is-odd@3.0.1  → score 100/100  ALLOW  (signed, no install scripts)</span></code></pre>
    </Panel>
    <p class="note">
      This transcript shows a block policy. The production deployment at
      Codento Engine runs in observe mode and blocks nothing yet.
    </p>

    <h3>The parts</h3>
    <dl class="rows">
      <div>
        <dt>Scoring</dt>
        <dd>
          The audit engine starts at 100, subtracts a weighted penalty per
          finding and forces a block on any critical finding. An LLM adapter
          can add context to a verdict. It never changes the score.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Policy and approval</dt>
        <dd>
          Per-enterprise policy is signed, with a lint and a dry-run impact
          preview against real audit history before signing. Capability
          manifests declare what a package may do at install time. The agent
          can request an approval and only a human can grant it, enforced by
          signed Ed25519 role tokens on the control plane.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Sandbox</dt>
        <dd>
          Lifecycle scripts get no network, no credential environment
          variables, no writes outside a fixed floor, no <code>$HOME</code>
          reads, and cannot execute binaries outside an approved floor. Each
          denial is lifted only by an approved capability. A detected
          violation can quarantine that exact tarball fleet-wide.
          <code>sentinel exec</code> runs any command under the same sandbox,
          without a shell and with argument boundaries preserved.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Publishing and resolution</dt>
        <dd>
          The publish route is npm-compatible. Every version is scored before
          it is accepted and commits atomically. A deterministic
          native-versus-mirror rule serves a claimed name only from
          Sentinel's own store, so public npm cannot shadow it. That closes
          dependency confusion for claimed names.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Namespace claims</dt>
        <dd>
          Ownership is proven by a DNS TXT challenge on the organization's
          apex domain and shipped as a signed, offline claim corpus verified
          at boot. Claims renew yearly and freeze on failure. A lapsed name
          never falls through to public npm and never transfers
          automatically to a domain's new owner.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Retraction</dt>
        <dd>
          Past 72 hours or 1,000 cumulative downloads, a release is
          immutable. A retracted version leaves a 410 tombstone, its
          identifier is permanently spent, and a retraction advisory flags
          any lockfile that still pins it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Release cooldown</dt>
        <dd>
          Policy can hold freshly published versions for a wall-clock window
          before installers see them. This counters attacks that ship a
          trojaned release and rely on installs within hours. Last-known-good
          suggestions respect the hold.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Detection</dt>
        <dd>
          Offline signature and Sigstore provenance verification,
          known-malicious (GHSA) advisories and CVE-range detection, typosquat
          and dependency-confusion detection, and release-anomaly findings:
          maintainer takeover, dormancy resurrection and new capabilities in
          a patch release. Raw-byte magic classification and loader-chain
          analysis catch binaries shipped as source files.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Remediation</dt>
        <dd>
          <code>sentinel explain</code> turns a verdict into per-finding
          remediation and a suggested last-known-good version. It is
          advisory and never rewrites a lockfile.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Installers</dt>
        <dd>
          npm, pnpm, Yarn (1 and Berry) and bun work unmodified against the
          registry. Leaving registry mode requires an explicit
          acknowledgement and emits a revert manifest, so claimed names
          cannot silently flip back to public npm.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>History and metrics</dt>
        <dd>
          Durable history is opt-in and stored in SQLite with no extra
          dependency. It gives verdict trends, top-flagged packages and a
          violation timeline on the dashboard, the CLI and
          <code>/-/metrics</code>.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </Section>

  <Section id="security" title="Security and limits" note="Sentinel is a pre-1.0 alpha. Read this before you rely on it.">
    <dl class="rows">
      <div>
        <dt>Alpha</dt>
        <dd>Sentinel is pre-1.0 and not production hardened. Its APIs may change.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Observe mode in production</dt>
        <dd>
          Codento Engine runs Sentinel in observe mode. It scores every
          tarball and blocks none yet, so enforcement (block) mode is not
          yet proven in production. In that deployment installs fail closed
          if the proxy is down, unless someone explicitly bypasses it. No
          one outside Codento Engine runs Sentinel in production.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Unreleased fixes</dt>
        <dd>
          The November 2026 security-audit fix round (#84) and install-hook
          script following (#89) are on <code>main</code>. Both came after
          0.1.0-alpha.2 and are not in a release yet.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Evidence still to collect</dt>
        <dd>The next milestone is design-partner evidence: blocked incidents, false-positive rate and audit latency.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Opt-in behavior</dt>
        <dd>Auto-quarantine is opt-in and auth-gated. Rate limits on expensive routes are opt-in.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Network trust boundary</dt>
        <dd>Tarball fetches are pinned to allowlisted origins to prevent SSRF, Host-header spoofing is refused, and expensive routes have byte caps.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p class="note">
      The <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/sentinel-threat-model.md`}>threat model</a>
      and the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}>security policy</a>
      have the details.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="changes" title="Recent changes" note="November 2026">
    <div class="changes">
      <h3>November 2026</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Sentinel runs in production at Codento Engine as the auditing npm proxy in the build stack, in observe mode.</li>
        <li>On 20 November a security-audit fix round (#84) merged to <code>main</code>.</li>
        <li>The audit now follows install hooks that run npm, pnpm, yarn or bun scripts (#89).</li>
        <li>#84 and #89 came after 0.1.0-alpha.2 and are not in that release yet.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Since July 2026</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Obfuscation false-positive fixes, approval identity coverage and dependency updates.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>July 2026</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>0.1.0-alpha.2 was released on 14 July 2026.</li>
        <li>
          The registry work (Phases 30 to 33, ADRs 0045 to 0048) was
          implemented as of 13 July 2026: audit-gated publishes, verified
          namespace claims, time-locked retraction, and a registry that npm,
          pnpm, Yarn and bun can use, with a safe exit path.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Project>
